9.2 Hot Standby Router Protocol (HSRP)
6. HSRP Overview

I learnt that Hot Standby Router Protocol (HSRP) provides gateway redundancy by allowing multiple routers to share a single virtual IP address. I understood that:

Hosts use the virtual IP address as their default gateway.
Only one router actively forwards traffic.
A standby router automatically assumes control if the active router fails.

This demonstrated how HSRP provides seamless network continuity.

7. HSRP Priority and Preemption

I learnt how HSRP determines which router becomes active.

The router with the highest priority becomes the active router.
Preemption allows a higher-priority router to regain the active role after recovering from a failure.

This helped me understand how administrators control HSRP elections.

8. HSRP States and Timers

I learnt the different operational states used by HSRP, including:

Initial
Learn
Listen
Speak
Standby
Active

I also learnt that HSRP uses timers to:

Detect router failures.
Maintain communication between routers.
Ensure quick failover when required.

This strengthened my understanding of HSRP operation.

